			<div class="content" id="post-content-4468902">
									

<p>This might work for those who aren't well-versed in this particular aspect of wiki lore, but for me, there isn't a whole lot of intruigue to be had here.</p>
<p>The base idea of your subject is definitely one that's worth pursuing &#8212; I'm not against it remotely, but it's too bare-bones for me to truly become invested in it. Short articles work for me when there's something very novel, unique, or interesting to make it stand out; evocative tableaus concentrating on imagery or aiming to evoke a specific emotion are also an excellent way to use brevity to your advantage.</p>
<p>This is falling just short of each of those attributes. It's shining light on an established piece of site lore (one that, again, I would like to see explored), so it's not quite going out there in terms of originality&#8230; and, despite having the <em>potential</em> to evoke strong emotions or paint a memorable picture, it gets a touch distracted in detailing supplemental details and political events that don't meaningfully contribute to the moment the mystery is built upon.</p>

							</div>

								
					
